#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120976} :activity/id #uuid "87f0cf5a-7b6f-5f30-9395-ce15661f7f42" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049663 :topic/id #uuid "091d7993-283f-5939-84c7-ddae5972ba72" :topic/math-academy-id 1744} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119879 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119698 :question/id #uuid "40927582-03c4-5dcb-b2cc-7c68c73388b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8688"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eb7fd4f-f4bc-535e-b6bf-0c7df387a375" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048563}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395458}] :knowledge-point/title "Reversing the Limits of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "b9eea760-99ae-5d7b-82d5-ea90245e119d" :step/math-academy-id 32283 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121079 :tutorial/content "Given a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ that is continuous on a closed interval $[a,b],$ the *rule for reversal of integration limits* states the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{b}^{a}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "43078f08-37bf-59a3-8462-3b3efc865eb1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5177 :tutorial/title "The Rule for Reversal of Integration Limits"} :step/id #uuid "660b20a9-f073-51b0-a1b7-e72f161ebfb3" :step/math-academy-id 32530 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120789}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121080 :tutorial/content "Given a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ that is continuous on a closed interval $[a,b]$ and $p \\in (a,b),$ the *adjacent intervals rule* states the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{a}^{p}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{p}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ef0617f3-7ee0-5300-8795-10adbdc19d3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5178 :tutorial/title "The Adjacent Intervals Rule"} :step/id #uuid "84aafb0e-a1fc-506a-aa26-8b23e04c1756" :step/math-academy-id 32531 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121081 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf f(t)$ is a vector-valued function that is continuous on a closed interval $[a,b]$ and $\\mathbf c$ is a constant vector. \nThen, the *products rules* are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}[c\\cdot f(t)]\\text{d}t=c\\cdot ({\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t)\\, & \\text{dot product rule} \\\\ {\\int }_{a}^{b}[c\\times f(t)]\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\, & \\text{cross product rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2531e67a-b5ed-5052-a97f-34abca386c58" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5179 :tutorial/title "The Product Rules"} :step/id #uuid "60fca522-e8ee-530d-9936-cc7ec084b35f" :step/math-academy-id 32532 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120979}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120995 :tutorial/content "Since the integration of vector functions is done component-by-component, the properties of integrals of vector functions are immediately inherited from those of the integrals of real-valued functions. \n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are vector-valued functions that are continuous on a closed interval $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha$ is a scalar. \nThen, the *vector addition and scalar multiplication rules* are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}(f(t)+g(t))\\text{d}t={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,+\\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\, & \\text{vector addition rule} \\\\ {\\int }_{a}^{b}\\alpha \\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\alpha \\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\, & \\text{scalar constant multiple rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, if $\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: \\cos t\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf g(t) = \\left\\langle -t^2, \\: \\sin t \\right\\rangle,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int [f(t)+2g(t)]\\text{d}t & =\\int f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+2\\int g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\,\\text{d}t+2\\int ⟨-{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{cos}t\\,\\text{d}t⟩+⟨2\\int -{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,2\\int \\operatorname{sin}t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}t+{C}_{2}⟩+⟨-\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{3},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}t+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3{t}^{2}-4{t}^{3}}{6},\\,\\operatorname{sin}t-2\\operatorname{cos}t⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C_1, \\, C_2, \\, C_3, \\, C_4$ are arbitrary real constants and ${\\mathbf C} = \\langle C_1+C_3, C_2+C_4 \\rangle$ is a constant vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "b3d07bd2-8d66-5645-a140-c638f5e9f002" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2342 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a65b687f-5aa2-5d59-93d7-c89fdaa5d539" :step/math-academy-id 9874 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120977}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119980 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119550 :question/id #uuid "b25256b2-09fe-5faa-b27a-bfea7654e8ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-3019"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0333b70a-2ede-5c8d-b298-4d25ae4d4667" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049202}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362269} 